"answer": "It is normal to become a bit more forgetful as you age, such as occasionally forgetting names or where items are placed [MLP-1452][MLP-205]. However, dementia is not a normal part of aging; it is a serious disorder that interferes with daily life and activities [MLP-205]. While mild forgetfulness can be typical, forgetting how to use familiar objects or getting lost in known places may indicate a more serious problem like Alzheimer's disease or other types of dementia [MLP-1452][MLP-205]. If you are concerned about your memory changes, consulting a healthcare provider is recommended to distinguish between normal aging and potential cognitive impairment [MLP-1452][MLP-22]."
}
